{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2957707c-fdb0-440a-9712-eabf816c9d7f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Goal**: This pipeline aims to answer the following biological question: Whether these correlated enhancers of a given gene are driven by TEs (i.e., overlapped with TEs). In addition, this pipeline also tests if highly correlated enhancers related to a given gene would form a network or networks.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **Example data**: The testing dataset is a small mouse brain single-cell multiome dataset (221.2 MB) and can be downloaded from 10X Genomics website (https://www.10xgenomics.com/datasets/fresh-embryonic-e-18-mouse-brain-5-k-1-standard-1-0-0). Key metrics of the dataset can be found on the website. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### We will use the processed data for testing the pipeline. Information about cell barcodes, features (gene names and locations, as well as peak locations) can be found in the folder named \"filtered_feature_bc_matrix\". "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7244858d-f467-4b54-b27c-f6c3f4f35ffe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Pipeline walkthrough: All, except step 1, are implemented in python >= 3.7\n",
    "#### Step 1 (done in R): Using the Cicero library to quantify co-accessibility scores of peaks near the gene of interest.\n",
    "```r\n",
    "library(monocle3)\n",
    "library(cicero)\n",
    "library(Matrix)\n",
    "\n",
    "indata <- readMM(paste(data_path,\"filtered_feature_bc_matrix/matrix.mtx.gz\", sep=\"\", collapse=NULL))\n",
    "# binarize the matrix\n",
    "indata@x[indata@x > 0] <- 1\n",
    "\n",
    "# format cell info\n",
    "cellinfo <- read.table(paste(data_path, \"filtered_feature_bc_matrix/barcodes.tsv.gz\", sep=\"\", collapse=NULL))\n",
    "row.names(cellinfo) <- cellinfo$V1\n",
    "names(cellinfo) <- \"cells\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# get features\n",
    "features=read.table(paste(data_path, \"filtered_feature_bc_matrix/features.tsv.gz\", sep=\"\", collapse=NULL), header=F,\n",
    "                    row.names=1, sep=\"\\t\")\n",
    "peaks_only <- rownames(features)[features$V3 ==\"Peaks\"]\n",
    "gene_only <- rownames(features)[features$V3== \"Gene Expression\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "#Format peak info\n",
    "peakinfo <- read.table(paste(data_path,\"filtered_feature_bc_matrix/peaks.bed\", sep=\"\", collapse=NULL))\n",
    "names(peakinfo) <- c(\"chr\", \"bp1\", \"bp2\")\n",
    "peakinfo$site_name <- paste(peakinfo$chr, peakinfo$bp1, peakinfo$bp2, sep=\"_\")\n",
    "peakinfo$site_name <- sub(\"_\", \":\", peakinfo$site_name, fixed=T)\n",
    "peakinfo$site_name <- sub(\"_\", \"-\", peakinfo$site_name, fixed=T)\n",
    "row.names(peakinfo) <- peakinfo$site_name\n",
    "\n",
    "rownames(indata) <- row.names(features)\n",
    "colnames(indata) <- row.names(cellinfo)\n",
    "\n",
    "# only use rows that are peaks\n",
    "new_indata <- indata[peaks_only, ]\n",
    "input_cds = suppressWarnings(new_cell_data_set(new_indata, cell_metadata = cellinfo, gene_metadata = peakinfo))\n",
    "\n",
    "# ensure no peaks included with zero reads\n",
    "input_cds <- input_cds[Matrix::rowSums(exprs(input_cds)) !=0,]\n",
    "\n",
    "set.seed(2017)\n",
    "input_cds <- monocle3::detect_genes(input_cds)\n",
    "input_cds <- estimate_size_factors(input_cds)\n",
    "input_cds <- preprocess_cds(input_cds, method=\"LSI\")\n",
    "input_cds <- reduce_dimension(input_cds, reduction_method = 'UMAP', preprocess_method = \"LSI\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# plot umap\n",
    "plot_cells(input_cds)                                                                                  \n",
    "```"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "40e77e55-751d-4dfe-95dd-e18cdbc895c6.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4e7cc4a5-f889-4cd4-89a6-d16fd107dfc9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "![cicero_umap.png](attachment:40e77e55-751d-4dfe-95dd-e18cdbc895c6.png)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e49ff38d-ce54-436b-9ffd-896896fddd66",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Next, we prepare a cicero data object and plot the connections between peaks for a selected gene\n",
    "```r\n",
    "umap_coords <- reducedDims(input_cds)$UMAP\n",
    "# make a cicero data object using the umap coordinates\n",
    "cicero_cds <- make_cicero_cds(input_cds, reduced_coordinates = umap_coords)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Here we use a mouse ATAC-seq data from 10X multiome as input, so we go with the mouse genom.\n",
    "# Change to the respective genome if data is of a different species\n",
    "data(\"mouse.mm9.genome\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# use only a small part of the genome and a small sample size to test the connections between peaks\n",
    "# Specify the chromosome where the target gene (e.g., chromosome 3 for Macrod) locates\n",
    "sample_genome <- subset(mouse.mm9.genome, V1 == \"chr2\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# run on 2 samples\n",
    "conns <- run_cicero(cicero_cds, sample_genome, sample_num = 2)\n",
    "conns_sub <- conns[conns$coaccess >=0.2, ]\n",
    "# drop nan\n",
    "comp_cases=conns_sub[complete.cases(conns_sub), ]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualizing Cicero Connections for chr 2\n",
    "# Download the GTF associated with this data (mm9) from ensembl and load it\n",
    "# using rtracklayer\n",
    "# download and unzip\n",
    "temp <- tempfile()\n",
    "download.file(\"ftp://ftp.ensembl.org/pub/release-65/gtf/mus_musculus/Mus_musculus.NCBIM37.65.gtf.gz\", temp)\n",
    "gene_anno <- rtracklayer::readGFF(temp)\n",
    "unlink(temp)\n",
    "\n",
    "# rename some columns to match requirements\n",
    "gene_anno$chromosome <- paste0(\"chr\", gene_anno$seqid)\n",
    "gene_anno$gene <- gene_anno$gene_id\n",
    "gene_anno$transcript <- gene_anno$transcript_id\n",
    "gene_anno$symbol <- gene_anno$gene_name\n",
    "plot_connections(conns, \"chr2\", 140000000, 140856988,\n",
    "                 gene_model = gene_anno,\n",
    "                 coaccess_cutoff = .2,\n",
    "                 connection_width = .5, peak_color=\"purple\", gene_model_color=\"green\",\n",
    "                 collapseTranscripts = \"longest\" )\n",
    "\n",
    "```"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "45c4d168-efcf-4b67-afd8-d0dea9da4b4b.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b4464c87-76c3-4dd5-8728-d08d2e8299c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "![plot_cicero_tracks_chr2.png](attachment:45c4d168-efcf-4b67-afd8-d0dea9da4b4b.png)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "66960765-2b72-416e-a7f0-5f7f470e8b90",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finally, we run cicero and save those enhancer-pair with co-accessibility scores >= 0.2 \n",
    "```r\n",
    "# Use a large samples and the whole genome for real\n",
    "conns <- run_cicero(cicero_cds, mouse.mm9.genome, sample_num = 100)\n",
    "# select connections with co-accessibility scores >= 0.2\n",
    "conns_sub <- conns[conns$coaccess >=0.2, ]\n",
    "# save the subset data for the sunsequent step\n",
    "write.csv(conns, \"results/whole_genome_coaccess_gt0.2.csv\", row.names=FALSE)\n",
    "\n",
    "```"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "71b3c1b6-7a59-43c4-a577-a222e7e77313",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### The following steps are done in python, so import all the needed packages first"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "4037b479-d648-431c-bf47-743dbb96c296",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import random\n",
    "from pybedtools import BedTool\n",
    "import scipy.sparse\n",
    "from fuc import pybed\n",
    "from scipy.io import mmwrite\n",
    "from scipy.sparse import csr_matrix\n",
    "from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n",
    "from collections import defaultdict\n",
    "import os\n",
    "import glob\n",
    "import csv\n",
    "import gzip\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import network_visualization as nv\n",
    "import steamer_enhancer as se"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c0aeea50-e534-490d-a104-5164ca802d6e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 2: Construct networks for highly correlated enhancers related to a pre-selected target gene"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "4937ee3d-9fe3-4aaa-8495-4e22ce602dba",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Gene of interest: MACROD2, a proten-coding gene associated with disease, such as hypogonadotropic hypogonadism 21 and autism.\n",
    "# MACROD2 is located at chromosome 2, 140395308-140395595\n",
    "# select enhancers locate within 500kn up- and down-stream of MACROD2 and with co-accessibility score >= 0.2\n",
    "\n",
    "output_dir = 'results/'\n",
    "os.makedirs(output_dir,exist_ok=True)\n",
    "#whole_gem=pd.read_csv(\"results/whole_genome_gt0.2.csv\")\n",
    "df=pd.read_csv(\"results/whole_genome_gt0.2.csv\").dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "# extract correlated enhacners of the target gene, Macrod 2 (on chromosome)\n",
    "macrod2_df=df[df[\"Peak1\"].str.contains(\"chr2\")]\n",
    "macrod2_df.to_csv(\"results/marods2_enhancer_coaccess_gt0.2.csv\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# concatenate vertically these enhacners to the dataframe\n",
    "new_df = se.get_enhancers(\"results/marods2_enhancer_coaccess_gt0.2.csv\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# preprare significant enhacners in the bed format\n",
    "nearby_enhancers, barcode= se.get_nearby_enhancers(new_df, \"chr2\", 140395308, 140395595)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "276b9fe7-7012-42b3-aae2-648a5197a13d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 3:Visualize networks"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "3d4cbcc4-c17f-45f8-b92a-ddcfe497718f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1440x1170 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Since there are still too many enhancer-pairs that makes network visualization difficult, we will only look at strongly correlated enhancer pairs\n",
    "# The definition of strongly correlated enhancer pairs will be provided by users as min_high_corr, e.g., 0.8\n",
    "\n",
    "filename=\"results/marods2_enhancer_coaccess_gt0.2.csv\"\n",
    "nv.network_polt(filename, 0.8)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "557a7746-d812-43de-b1e8-0ba64f949fe1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 4: Prepare TE annotation file (in .bed) and intersect TEs and nearby_enhancers bed files"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b9788e7d-9aa8-474b-b18c-3bd023758230",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "TEs=se.create_bed_for_TEs(\"mm10.nrph.hits.gz\")\n",
    "# read in TEs.bed and enhancer bed file, Frag.bed, which was generated in the previous step\n",
    "TEs=BedTool(\"results/TEs.bed\")\n",
    "frags=BedTool(\"results/Frag.bed\")\n",
    "intersected_bed=se.intersection(TEs, frags)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "97ab100f-3f86-42a3-a5f5-15a74ab66585",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 5: Compute TE-by-enhancer matrices"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 32,
   "id": "0dd85852-5d3e-4361-9d39-cc37500ea34a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Number of enahcners present in the TE-by-enhancer matrix: (220, 3)\n",
      "Files saved to TE_Fams_matrix directory\n",
      "Files saved to TE_Unique_matrix directory\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "barcode=pd.read_csv(\"results/Frag.bed\", sep=\"\\t\", header=None).iloc[:, 3]\n",
    "dfam=pd.read_table(\"mm10.nrph.hits.gz\", sep=\"\\t\")\n",
    "TE_fams=dfam[\"family_name\"].unique().tolist()\n",
    "\n",
    "unique_TEs_sparse_matrix,TE_Fams_sparse_matrix,unique_TEs_list,TEs_fams,barcode_dict=se.make_cell_x_element_matrix(intersected_bed, barcode)\n",
    "print(f\"Number of enahcners present in the TE-by-enhancer matrix: {unique_TEs_sparse_matrix.shape}\")\n",
    "# save as sparse matrices\n",
    "unique_TEs_sparse_matrix = se.convert_df_to_sparse(unique_TEs_sparse_matrix)\n",
    "TE_Fams_sparse_matrix = se.convert_df_to_sparse(TE_Fams_sparse_matrix)\n",
    "\n",
    "# save as 10X-like data format\n",
    "output_dir = 'results/TE_Fam_matrix/'\n",
    "file_path = 'barcodes.tsv'\n",
    "\n",
    "output_dir = 'results/TE_Fam_matrix/'\n",
    "os.makedirs(output_dir,exist_ok=True)\n",
    "#start saving barcode info\n",
    "file_path = 'barcodes.tsv'\n",
    "#se.compress_tsv_file(file_path,output_dir,barcode_dict)\n",
    "#starting saving the features\n",
    "df = se.make_features_df(TEs_fams)\n",
    "path = 'results/TE_Fam_matrix/features.tsv.gz'\n",
    "se.save_df_as_gz(df, path)\n",
    "#start saving the matrix\n",
    "file_path = 'results/TE_Fam_matrix/matrix.mtx'\n",
    "se.compress_sparse_matrix(TE_Fams_sparse_matrix,file_path)\n",
    "print('Files saved to TE_Fams_matrix directory')\n",
    "#start on the unique TE info\n",
    "output_dir = 'results/TE_Unique_matrix/'\n",
    "os.makedirs(output_dir,exist_ok=True)\n",
    "#start saving barcode info\n",
    "file_path = 'barcodes.tsv'\n",
    "se.compress_tsv_file(file_path,output_dir,barcode_dict)\n",
    "#start saving the features\n",
    "df = se.make_features_df(unique_TEs_list)\n",
    "path = 'results/TE_Unique_matrix/features.tsv.gz'\n",
    "se.save_df_as_gz(df,path)\n",
    "#start saving the matrix\n",
    "file_path = 'results/TE_Unique_matrix/matrix.mtx'\n",
    "se.compress_sparse_matrix(unique_TEs_sparse_matrix,file_path)\n",
    "print('Files saved to TE_Unique_matrix directory')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ca75a82e-b4b8-44cb-8f9f-746083159a5b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Summary (answer the biological questions and draw a conclusion if possible)\n",
    "#### This tutorial takes a single-cell multiome mouse brain data as input and yields the following findings:\n",
    "#### 1. As expected, some significant enhancers are overlapped with TE sequences.\n",
    "#### 2. Although most enhancer-enhancer connections are unique, there are a few of enhancers that connect with more than one enhancers and thus form a small network.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Conclusion: \n",
    "#### TEs are knwon for their abundance and repetition in the genome. Fidnings from the mouse brain data not only are in line with this view, but further support that signifincant enhancers of a given gene (e.g., MACROD2 in this tutorial) can overlap with TEs. In addition, some of the significant enhancers connect with multiple enhancers and thus form small networks. The enhancer that has more than one connection in each netowrk is often considered as the hub, hinting its more influential role in gene expression."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "metadata": {
  "kernelspec": {
   "display_name": "Python 3 (ipykernel)",
   "language": "python",
   "name": "python3"
  },
